<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Terence</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 159 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Also known as Publius Terentius Afer</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Terence, in full Publius Terentius Afer, was a Roman comic playwright, said to have been born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> as a slave. His six surviving comedies, adapted from Greek New Comedy, are known for refined language and careful characterisation. He died young around 159 BCE, and his plays were widely read in schools for centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/terence.jpg" alt="Terence" width="320" height="319" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Terence_from_Vaticana,_Vat._lat.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ancient authors say that Terence was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> as a slave in the household of an otherwise unknown senator, P. Terentius Lucanus, who educated and freed him, after which he took the name Terentius. He is said to have become a member of the Scipionic Circle. This biography derives from the Vita Terenti preserved in the commentary of Aelius Donatus and attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suetonius">Suetonius</a>, and it is often construed as conjecture based on the plays and their production notices. His cognomen Afer, meaning the African, may be the source of the report of African birth, since such names did not necessarily denote origin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Terence wrote six comedies based on Greek originals by Menander or Apollodorus of Carystus, and all six survive complete. According to the production notices, each was staged by the company of Lucius Ambivius Turpio, with music by a tibicen named Flaccus. Andria was produced in 166 BCE, Heauton timorumenos in 163, Eunuchus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phormio">Phormio</a> in 161, and Adelphoe and Hecyra in 160, the last after two abortive productions. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> had no permanent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> before 55 BCE, so the plays were staged on temporary wooden stages for audiences of fewer than 2,000.</p><p>Unlike <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plautus">Plautus</a>, Terence favoured simple, conversational <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> over visual humour. Five of the six plays centre on a pair of young men in love. In his prologues he abandoned the traditional explanation of the plot and instead answered criticism, aimed at an old and spiteful poet whom Donatus identified as Luscius Lanuvinus.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>According to tradition, Terence sailed east around the age of 25 and died either of disease in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> or in a shipwreck on the return. His plays quickly became school texts, and by the end of the 2nd century BCE he was a literary classic. By the late 4th century CE he was one of four canonical school authors, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sallust">Sallust</a> and Vergil, and he kept a central place in the European curriculum until the 19th century, influencing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-shakespeare">William Shakespeare</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moliere">Molière</a>. The Codex Bembinus, kept in the Vatican library, dates to the 4th or early 5th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Terence” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Contact with the Greek world transformed Roman culture: Livius Andronicus translated the Odyssey into Latin around 240 BCE, Plautus and <b>Terence</b> adapted Greek comedy, Ennius wrote the epic Annales, and in the late Republic Cicero, Lucretius, Catullus, Sallust and Caesar created a classical Latin prose and poetry.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Livius Andronicus, a Greek from southern Italy, translated the Odyssey and staged the first Latin play adapted from a Greek original in 240 BCE; Plautus and <b>Terence</b> wrote comedies, and Ennius wrote an epic history of Rome in Greek metre.</blockquote><a href="/language/latin">Latin · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/tunisia">Tunisia</a>. Died in Lake Stymphalia, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.85,10.31&amp;year=-169" aria-label="Carthage on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4809 836 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Terence"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5259 1106h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5259 1106h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.85,10.31&amp;year=-169">What was here in 169 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I am a man, and feel for all mankind.&#8221;</p><footer>The Self-Tormentor, act 1 &#183; tr. George Colman, 1765 &#183; spoken by the old farmer Chremes to a neighbour who tells him to mind his own business &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/22695/pg22695-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Quarrels of lovers but renew their love.&#8221;</p><footer>The Woman of Andros, act 3 &#183; tr. George Colman, 1765 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/22695/pg22695-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;In fine, nothing is said now that has not been said before.&#8221;</p><footer>The Eunuch, prologue &#183; tr. Henry T. Riley, 1853 &#183; answering critics who accused him of borrowing from older plays &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/22188/pg22188-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
